t. Among all beneficiaries, receipt of serology tests decreased as neighborhood disadvantage increased (Chi-squared p=0.001). Annual serology tests were half as common among patients residing in the most disadvantaged neighborhood quintile compared to patients in the least disadvantaged neighborhoods (20.7% vs 39.6%, p<0.0001; Figure 3B ). When stratified by race and ethnicity, receipt of serology tests decreased with increasing neighborhood disadvantage among Black and White patients (both p=0.03). There were no differences across levels of neighborhood disadvantage among Hispanic beneficiaries and beneficiaries in the other race and ethnicity category. Odds of having annual serology tests were higher among female patients (OR 1.78; 1.08, 2.92; Table 3 ) and those of Hispanic ethnicity (1.98; 1.33, 2.93), prior to adjustment. Both ESKD (0.72; 0.53, 0.98) and neurologic disorders (0.32; 0.16, 0.62) were associated with lower receipt of serology tests. Having one to four primary care visits (2.08; 1.33, 3.24) or five or more primary care visits (2.26; 1.45, 3.51) were similarly associated with increased odds of serologies. Stronger associations were seen with having one or two rheumatology visits (3.67; 2.38, 5.66) or three or more rheumatology visits (7.51; 5.03, 11.23). Table 3. Observed Percentage and Unadjusted and Adjusted Odds Ratios for Receipt of Annual Lupus-Specific Serology Test among Young Adults with SLE Patient Characteristic Observed % (95% CI) Unadjusted Odds Ratio OR (95% CI) Adjusted Odds Ratio aOR (95% CI) Age (per decade) - 0.91 (0.64, 1.31) 0.92 (0.61, 1.39) Sex Female 29.3 (26.4, 32.2) 1.78 (1.08, 2.92) 1.52 (0.87, 2.65) Race and ethnicity White 24.0 (19.3, 28.8) ref ref Black 26.2 (22.1, 30.3) 1.12 (0.80, 1.57) 1.19 (0.81, 1.76) Hispanic 38.5 (31.4, 45.5) 1.98 (1.33, 2.93) 2.00 (1.26, 3.19) Other 31.6 (22.2, 40.9) 1.46 (0.88, 2.52) 1.29 (0.73, 2.29) Neighborhood disadvantage (per ADI quintile) - 0.81 (0.74, 0.90) 0.80 (0.71, 0.90) RUCA Suburban