s increased the odds even further to nearly seven-fold (6.97; 4.56, 10.67). The presence of a primary care visit nearly doubled the odds of having serology tests (1.85; 1.13, 3.03), while the odds did not increase with more primary care visits (1.89; 1.15, 3.12). In sensitivity analyses, inclusion of comorbidity indicators that were non-significant did not substantively alter odds ratios. Results stratified by baseline ESKD status are displayed in Supplementary Table 2 . Findings show that ADI remains strongly associated with lower odds of serologic testing (0.78; 0.68, 0.89) and regional variation remains significant with and without ESKD. DISCUSSION In this study of young adults with lupus, only 39% were retained in lupus care based on rheumatology visits and just 28% had a lupus-specific serology test in the last year, despite even more stringent recommendations in clinical guidelines for patients with lupus 23 . Visit-based retention was 26% lower for patients from the most disadvantaged neighborhoods compared to the least disadvantaged. For serology tests, even after adjusting for primary care and rheumatology utilization in a uniformly insured cohort, higher neighborhood disadvantage was tightly associated with lower odds of serology testing. Half as many patients from the most disadvantaged neighborhoods had an annual serology test compared to patients from the least disadvantaged areas. Seeing a rheumatologist appeared crucial to receiving lupus-specific serologies, increasing odds at least three-fold. Considerable regional variation in serology testing was present throughout the US with over 50% lower odds in the Midwest compared to the South, although there were no differences for visit-based retention or serology testing for rural patients. Notable findings appeared by simultaneously examining race and ethnicity with neighborhood disadvantage. White patients from the least disadvantaged neighborhoods particularly appear to benefit with higher visit-based retention than any other group of patients. Conversely, Black patients had only half the visit-based retention of White patients in the least disadvantaged neighborhoods. This finding is consistent with the ‘diminished returns’ phenomenon in which the positive health effects of socioeconomic and other resources are systematically smaller for Black patients than White patients in the US 42 . Such diminished effects have been found for SES, education, and employment on outcomes including prevalence of chronic disease and mortality, with additional impacts of racism beyond SES and income speculated to be key mechanisms 42 . Another notable finding was higher odds of serology testing among Hispanic patients. Similarly, lower mortality has been reported for Hispanic Medicaid beneficiaries with lupus 43 . Differences in social support patterns are hypothesized to play a role in these outcome patterns 44 , 45 , with social support potentially superseding the influence of neighborhood disadvantage. Despite uniform insurance coverage and adjusting for number of primary care and rheumatology visits, patients from more disadvantaged neighborhoods had lower odds of receiving serology tests. For every quintile of neighborhood disadvantage, young adult lupus beneficiaries had 20% lower odds of annual lupus-specific serology testing. Given that serology testing is already recommended at least twice annually in clinical guidelines, receipt of at least an annual complement and/or dsDNA antibody test may be important metrics for monitoring disparities in lupus care quality given substantial gaps by neighborhood disadvantage, race and ethnicity, and US region. Comparing to prior literature, our observed 39% observed visit-based retention rate among young adult Medicare beneficiaries was lower than the 60–85% rate among adult patients in our prior assessments of visit-based retention in academic rheumatology clinics in two US cities 24 , 46 . In those studies, greater baseline healthcare system connection was required for initial inclusion – at least one primary care and one rheumatology ambulatory visit. In this Medicare cohort, 17% and 35% lacked such primary care and rheumatology visits, respectively, in the assessment period. Greater availability of rheumatology care in the cities studied in the prior literature versus nationally might contribute to different visit-retention rates, as we also observed significant variation in retention across the US. Regions with higher visit-based retention roughly corresponded to areas with greater per capita rheumatologists based on contemporaneous workforce studies 47 . This suggests support for calls to strengthen rheumatology provider access in underserved areas and/or expansion of telehealth options. While strengths of this study include a national, uniformly insured cohort of young adults with lupus, we also acknowledge limitations. First, since this study used claims data, there may be misclassification of SLE. We did use previously validated, published algorithms for administrative data to most accurately define SLE and other comorbidities 27 , 34 – 36 . Second, we were not able to account for functional status or current SLE disease activity which we acknowledge may impact visit and serology test frequency and appropriateness. Inclusion requirements of having a lupus-associated healthcare encounter and surviving for 18 months may also skew the cohort towards patients with some level of healthcare access, while excluding patients with high mortality risk or short life expectancies after qualifying for Medicare. Given that patients under age 65 qualify for Medicare based on ESKD or disability, the results from this cohort of young adult patients with lupus may not be generalizable to all young adults with lupus in the US, nor in other countries. However, the included young adult beneficiaries represent a particularly high-risk group of lupus patients for whom potential interventions to improve lupus care may be especially important. Another limitation is that while many lupus patients have kidney disease and may be principally followed by a nephrologist, nephrology visits were not considered as part of the definitions for visit-based retention and may account for the particularly low rate of visit-based retention in patients with ESKD. Additionally, advanced practice providers (e.g., nurse practitioners, physician assistants) working in rheumatology cannot be identified under current National Provider Identifier and Physician Specialty Code classifications 40 , 41 . Thus, some rheumatology advanced practice provider visits may not be included, underestimating visit-based retention. Similarly, as inpatient services can receive bundled payments, CPT codes for complement and dsDNA antibody tests may be missing if associated with a hospitalization. However, such labs may reflect testing in acute situations rather than chronic ambulatory disease monitoring. Further investigation of longitudinal retention in rheumatology care and receipt of lupus-specific serology tests in additional cohorts and among various age and payor groups is warranted. Likewise, it is not known if visit-based retention in lupus care and receipt of serology tests are associated with lupus outcomes and further research should evaluate whether these measures are important intervention targets. Crucially, contributors to the strong inverse association of neighborhood disadvantage with receipt of lupus-specific serology tests should be investigated to begin to develop strategies to achieve health equity. This study’s analysis of care process metrics in lupus can provide a baseline for evaluation of future interventions aimed at reducing disparities in care, while acknowledging multiple factors contribute to health disparities. Furthermore, our group is planning to investigate retention measures that include urinalysis and creatinine since the screening and monitoring of lupus nephritis are important aspects of new quality measures in lupus care. 48 In a US cohort of young adults with lupus, fewer than one in three had an annual lupus-specific complement or dsDNA antibody test, and only 39% saw a rheumatologist at least once every six months. Patients living in the most disadvantaged neighborhoods had 48% lower serology testing and 26% lower visit-based retention than patients from the least disadvantaged neighborhoods. These results reveal significant disparities in lupus care based on neighborhood-level disadvantage despite uniform insurance coverage. Regional variation in visit-based retention and serology testing was high, with lowest percentages in the Midwest, calling for further studies examining rheumatology access, practice patterns, and interventions. Together, the findings of this study suggest a critical need for greater evaluation of disparities in lupus care access and quality and how care differences may impact lupus outcome disparities in the US. Supplementary Material 1 NIHMS1983501-supplement-1.pdf (161.1KB, pdf) Funding Source: Primary sources of funding were the National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities Research awards F30MD015211 and R01MD010243. Additional support was provided by the University of Wisconsin Medical Scientist Training Program (NIH NIGMS T32 GM140935). Footnotes Declaration of Conflicting Interests CMB receives peer-reviewed institutional grant funding from Independent Grants for Learning and Change (Pfizer) for research unrelated to this study. All other authors declare no conflicts. REFERENCES 1. Panopalis P, Gillis JZ, Yazdany J, et al. Frequent use of the emergency department among persons with systemic lupus erythematosus. Arthritis Care Res (Hoboken). 2010; 62: 401–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Bartels CM, Chodara A, Chen Y, et al. One Quarter of Medicare Hospitalizations in Patients with Systemic Lupus Erythematosus Readmitted within Thirty Days. Semin Arthritis 