INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3967,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11349476','Implementing a Staff-Led Smoking Cessation Intervention in a Diverse Safety-Net Rheumatology Clinic: A Pre-Post Scalability Study in a Low-Resource Setting.','Brandt J, Ramly E, Lim SS, Bao G, Messina ML, Piper ME, Bartels CM.','2024','pamphlet wisc quitconnect rehearsed reboot restart programmer builder optum transmittal covariables sociodemographics sociodemographics unreachable unreachable unadj nnt nnt complains nonconcordant hiatus reboot unreachable caller supinfo nihms1985975 supinfo optum ul1tr002373 1k01hs028926 ramly avina zubieta sadatsafavi avina zubieta sadatsafavi lassere rappo portek manzi meilahn rairie crowson rollefstad ikdahl kremers crowson therneau esdaile abrahamowicz grodzicky sokka descalzo r30 gourraud cantagrel jagpal boeckler cosnes frances westhoff rau hyrich symmons sokolove lahey gladman urowitz parodis sjowall jonsen belimumab ezeh mckown jewell mccauliffe aimer stamp stebbings mayberry schappert rechtsteiner olivo buhr alcaraz voelker voelker demas trupin tonner myles r270 vreede wattiaux bettendorf wattiaux ramly podium fanouriakis kostopoulou alunno drosos vedder houben ramly panyard hipxchange quitconnecthealth ramly chodara ramly alvidrez castille laude ramly lauver bykovskyi ipaf ramly stroik lauver previsit creamer babb neff jaklevic e201 artiga presiado kirzinger kff kff battafarano ditmyer ulgatl sobg chaiton diemert e011045 supinfo nihms1985975 supinfo','Abstract Objectives Quit Connect ( QC ), our specialty clinic smoking cessation intervention, supports clinic staff to check, advise, and connect willing patients to a state quit line or class. QC improved tobacco screening and quit line referrals 26-fold in a predominantly White academic healthcare system population. Implementing QC includes education, electronic health record (EHR) reminders, and periodic audit feedback. This study tested QC’s feasibility and impact in a safety-net rheumatology clinic with a predominantly Black population. Methods In this pre-post study, adult rheumatology visits were analyzed 12 months pre- through 18 months post- QC intervention (November 2019 – November 2021, omitting COVID-19 peak April-Nov 2020). EHR data compared process and clinical outcomes, including offers, referrals to resources, completed referrals, and documented cessation. Clinic staff engaged in pre-post focus groups and questionnaires regarding intervention feasibility and acceptability. Cost effectiveness was also assessed. Results Visit-level patients who smoked were 89.8% Black and 69.5% women (n=550). Pre-intervention, clinic staff rarely asked patients about readiness to cut back smoking (< 10% assessment). Post QC intervention, staff assessed quit readiness in 31.8% of visits with patients who smoked (vs 8.1% pre); 58.9% of these endorsed readiness to cut back or quit. Of 102 accepting cessation services, 37% (n = 17) of those reached set a quit date. Staff found the intervention feasible and acceptable. Each quit attempt cost approximately $4–10. Conclusions In a safety-net rheumatology clinic with a predominantly Black population, QC improved tobacco screening, readiness-to-quit assessment, and referrals and was also feasible and cost-effective. Keywords: smoking cessation, quality improvement, CVD risk, rheumatic diseases Graphical Abstract: INTRODUCTION Patients with rheumatic diseases like rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) experience early cardiovascular disease (CVD) and death. 1 – 7 These health effects are in part related to the fact that up to 25% of rheumatology patients smoke. 8 In fact, smoking rates are often higher among persons with RA and SLE compared to peers without rheumatic disease. 9 – 11 In addition to smoking being a cardiovascular risk factor, it can increase disease severity and predict treatment-refractory disease for both RA 12 – 14 and SLE. 15 – 18 Yet a 2015 study reported that 64% of RA patients who smoke were unaware of relationships between smoking and worse RA severity or lower medication efficacy. 19 A recent study among female United States Veterans noted that the probability of a cessation medication prescription and/or behavior health referral for smoking cessation correlated with the number of tobacco screenings per year, pointing to the importance systematic screening and referral. 20 Rheumatology specialty clinic visits 21 are therefore vital to address reversible CVD risk factors, like smoking. Unfortunately, in a 2023 report, only one third of patients reported they were given advice or help to quit by their rheumatologist or any other health professionals. 22 We 23 – 25 and others 26 – 28 have shown that in specialty clinics, where up to 73% of RA and SLE visits occur, 21 only 1 in 10 visits addressed cessation. 29 When we engaged diverse patients with RA and SLE who smoke in focus groups, they voiced a limited knowledge of the effects of smoking on their rheumatology disease and despite an interest in quitting, they had little information on how to quit. 30 , 31 Smoking cessation support has been endorsed by the American College of Rheumatology (ACR) as a quality measure and is recommended in ACR and European guidelines for rheumatic diseases, 32 , 33 yet scalable interventions for rheumatology clinics are few. Quit Connect, 34 a specialty clinic staff-led smoking cessation intervention implemented by medical assistant staff and nurses, increased quit line referrals 26-fold in our prior study and took <90 seconds. 35 This initial validation of Quit Connect (QC) was conducted in a healthcare system connected to a large medical school where >85% of patients were White and 53.1% had private insurance. To further support broad implementation of the Quit Connect intervention, we tested its ability to increase cessation referrals in a safety-net rheumatology clinic with 100% Black staff. As the hospital already had a smoking cessation class in place to support smoking cessation efforts among patients, the addition of Quit Connect allowed comparison. Beyond extension of Quit Connect to a diverse population, the safety-net rheumatology implementation offered two natural experiments: 1) examining patient preference for a cessation class versus quit line support and 2) examining the impact of the pandemic. In addition, we were able to compare the results from the current study in a diverse, safety-net rheumatology clinic population to our prior results in a primarily White, commercially insured population. 35 , 36 METHODS Setting and Participants In this pre-post implementation study, the setting was a rheumatology clinic in Atlanta, Georgia in a public safety-net hospital affiliated with an academic medical center. Pre-pandemic, the rheumatology clinic conducted approximately 300 patient encounters per month. Eligible adult (≥18 years old) rheumatology visits from the intervention period (Nov 2019 – Nov 2021 omitting 6 pandemic months April-Nov 2020) were compared to pre-intervention baseline visits (Oct 2018 – Nov 2019) for tobacco screening and offers of referral to cessation resources upon positive screening. Study personnel (JB) manually abstracted baseline data including on 100 consecutive visits with people who smoke including rates of cessation advice and referrals 12 months pre-intervention to compare to 18 months post-intervention. Intervention The Quit Connect intervention uses electronic health record (EHR) prompts to encourage clinic staff to 1) Check tobacco use and 30-day readiness to quit at the time of initial triage, 2) Advise on CVD risk and the health benefits of smoking cessation, and 3) Connect willing patients to the quit line with an electronic referral order allowing the quit line to contact the patient directly. Staff were provided with coaching points to encourage patients to consider health and cost benefits of cessation. A pamphlet was provided to patients that provided information related to the state quit line. Staff entered referral for the quit line or class that would be signed by the provider during the visit. EHR referrals were sent directly to the quit line, and referred patients would receive an intake call from the quit line. Once contacted by the quit line, patients could receive coaching, consider setting a quit date or might be provided with nicotine patch or gum via mail to support their effort to quit. Patients who were contacted by the quit line also had the option to decline services. Implementation The on-site study team included rheumatology clinicians, supported by the principal investigator, a study coordinator, and multidisciplinary nursing and an implementation coinvestigator from another academic center. The targeted teams for implementing the Quit Connect intervention were the IT staff, who enabled the EHR implementation of the protocol, and the clinic staff of medical assistants (MA) and a registered nurse, who conducted the patient rooming process, smoking assessment and referrals. This study was reviewed by the Emory University Institutional Review Board and Grady Research Oversight Committee and deemed minimal risk with waiver of informed consent (Study ID: IRB 00–109541). Key components of the Quit Connect implementation, supported by the online toolkit ( https://hip.wisc.edu/project/quitconnect/ ), 34 included: 1) pre-implementation engagement meetings (20 minutes question and answer) with MA and clinic team staff, 2) education for the clinic room staff using the QC protocol, 3) EHR reminders within the workflow, and 4) monthly audit feedback for rooming staff on their protocol utilization. EHR reminders were built into the specialty clinic’s pre-visit rooming sequence to 1) standardize staff screening of tobacco use and readiness to quit within 30 days; 2) prompt staff to offer referral to cessation resources, including state tobacco quit line or health system cessation classes; and 3) interface with the quit line vendor to securely send referral orders to the quit line and receive reports of quit line results returned to the health system’s EHR. Clinic staff, including MAs and one RN, were educated on smoking cessation resources and rehearsed talking points for the evidence-based protocol use in a one-hour training session, and then were provided with monthly individual audit feedback data by a study physician in the clinic. Additionally, given unforeseen interruptions in implementing amid the COVID-19 pandemic, we offered reboot training in December 2020. We therefore compared performance pre-pandemic (Nov 2019-Mar 2020), during the COVID-19 peak (Apr 2020-Nov 2020), and upon restart of the project (Dec 2020-Apr 2021). At this rheumatology clinic, in addition to the state quit line resource, patients ready to quit were also offered referral to the hospital’s own smoking cessation classes because this was deemed important by system leaders who had previously implemented a separate process that was already in place at the time that our project was started. Therefore at clinic visits, patients were offered options to participate in an in-person class at the hospital or to utilize state-funded quit line resource as a remote and novel option. EHR Build and Reporting The study’s initial phase leveraged our published Quit Connect toolkit with its EHR build guide (and build supplement specific to Epic Corp EHR) to support the necessary EHR modifications. 34 The build guide provided instructions on how to create two staff reminders within the rooming sequence to: 1) ask readiness to quit and 2) offer referrals. Using the Quit Connect toolkit’s build guide, the clinic’s senior programmer builder coordinated with the state quit line via the vendor, Optum, to facilitate secure HL-7 interface transmittal of referrals from the clinic to the state quit line. The interface also securely delivered quit line results back to the clinic’s EHR (reach status, accepted resources, and quit date). This study also included monthly reports capturing process measures of the QC intervention, including staff-level percentages and graphical data regarding percentage of referrals and missed referral opportunities. The build was facilitated remotely through virtual platforms by two study leads over the course of six months prior to go-live and executed by an on-site IT analyst. After completion of build and reporting, the three IT staff members participated in a one-hour evaluative assessment with two study co-leads. Instructions for developing these reports were modified per their feedback. The next phase of implementation was a focus group that engaged clinic staff to tailor the intervention to their clinic’s workflow using toolkit resources. Following a short 20 minute virtual introductory call with clinic staff, we held a live one-hour in-person focus group at the clinic that included the two external study leads, the on-site rheumatologist co-leader, and the clinic’s core group of three medical assistants, one registered nurse, and the clinic administrator. Using a semi-structured staff interview guide, we assessed staff’s knowledge of cessation evidence, current workflows, and gathered feedback on the proposed workflow and patient education materials. These data were used tailor the QC intervention to the clinic’s workflow and to adapt the patient and staff education materials to be concordant with the clinic’s population and staff knowledge and acceptability. Prior to go-live, an hour-long hybrid interactive demonstration with the clinic rooming staff was conducted by the on-site rheumatologist in conjunction with the off-site principal investigator rheumatologist facilitating via virtual platform. Outcomes related to patient calls, reach status and quit dates, nicotine product usage were collected using data from the vendor in addition to data that was pushed to the EHR system. EHR-derived covariables included age, sex, race, ethnicity, comorbidity (rheumatoid arthritis, lupus, cardiovascular disease, diabetes mellitus, kidney disease, cancer, Charlson Comorbidity Index), and payer, to examine the sociodemographics and comorbidities according to the National Institutes on Minority Health and Health Disparities framework. 37 The race and ethnicity variables were acquired from the EHR field populated by patient report or the healthcare system (categories: Black, White Hispanic, White non-Hispanic, Other/Unknown which would include Asian ancestry or other groups). Staff Audit Feedback and Evaluations Reports on the audit and feedback process measures were provided to the MAs to promote protocol fidelity. 38 The on-site rheumatologist held brief one-on-one in-person monthly sessions with each staff member to review individual data, set goals, discuss barriers, and determine strategies for any identified barriers as well as suggestions for improvements to the protocol. Sessions averaged seven minutes. For the first three months of implementation, the one-on-one in-person sessions occurred monthly with each MA. There was an interruption in clinic in-person visits starting in month 4 with the COVID-19 pandemic disruptions. When regular operations resumed approximately seven months later, the in-person, one-on-one audit feedback sessions were held every other month after a 30-minute refresher session on the protocol and workflow. A one-hour, mid-point meeting with the MA team was conducted remotely 18 months post-intervention by the two study co-leads to gather evaluative feedback and suggestions for improvements. Anonymous 15-item staff questionnaires after the mid-point meeting assessed acceptability and adult learning domains (see Supplemental Table ). Analyses Sociodemographic data and process measures for assessing tobacco use and readiness to quit were compared (pre-post) using standard EHR documentation fields and new tagged data elements in the staff alerts and orders. We compared quit line referral events to historic abstracted performance to report percent improvement and odds ratios. Covariates included sociodemographics and comorbidities from the EHR that were significant in univariable models. Analyses compared pre-intervention and intervention implementation 11/2019 through 4/2021, which were manually abstracted using our previously published approaches. 29 We used chi-square tests to compare three key results pre- and post-implementation process measures and logistic regression to calculate unadjusted and adjusted odds ratios (OR) and 95% confidence intervals. Results were clustered by patient in this visit-level dataset. RESULTS Of the pre- and post-implementation visits, 13.1% and 14.3%, respectively, were with people who reported currently smoking and were included in the analyses ( Table 1 ). Post-intervention patients who attended the clinic visits and were included in the analyses were predominantly female (76.6%) and of Black race (79.5%). Almost all had Medicare (30.6%) or Medicaid (19.3%) insurance or were uninsured/unknown (39.0%); few visits had commercial insurance (11.1%). Approximately one third of post-intervention visits had RA (27.6%), and another approximately one third had SLE (34.0%). Approximately one in five had a history of cardiovascular disease (21.9%). Table 1. Description of visit-level patient characteristics pre and post-intervention implementation. Pre-intervention visits (n=4158) n (%) Post-intervention visits (n=3833) n (%) Pre- vs Post-Intervention Non-smokers n=3615 Smokers n=543 (13.1%) Non-smokers n=3283 Smokers n=550 (14.3%) Smokers p value Age (mean ± SD) 51.1 ± 14.6 53.5 ± 11.4 51.3 ± 14.7 52.7 ± 12.3 0.29 Sex Male 618 (17.1) 175 (32.2) 539 (16.4) 168 (30.5) 0.55 Female 2997 (82.9) 368 (67.8) 2744 (83.6) 382 (69.5) Race and Ethnicity Black 2832 (78.3) 477 (87.8) 2554 (77.8) 494 (89.8) 0.23 White Hispanic 504 (13.9) 19 (3.5) 480 (14.6) 10 (1.8) White non-Hispanic 141 (3.9) 38 (7.0) 134 (4.1) 41 (7.5) Other/Unknown 138 (3.8) 9 (1.7) 115 (3.5) 5 (0.9) Payer Commercial 339 (9.4) 33 (6.1) 393 (12.0) 32 (5.8) 0.99 Medicaid 708 (19.6) 155 (28.5) 579 (17.6) 161 (29.3) Medicare 1122 (31.0) 166 (30.6) 1007 (30.7) 167 (30.4) Unknown/Uninsured/Private Pay 1446 (40.0) 189 (34.8) 1304 (39.7) 190 (34.5) Rheumatoid Arthritis 1136 (31.6) 187 (34.6) 901 (27.7) 155 (28.4) 0.027 SLE 1256 (35.0) 161 (29.8) 1134 (34.9) 171 (31.3) 0.59 Cardiovascular Disease (CVD) * 859 (23.9) 115 (21.3) 744 (22.9) 94 (17.2) 0.088 Diabetes 985 (27.4) 125 (23.1) 883 (27.2) 112 (20.5) 0.29 Kidney Disease 777 (21.6) 97 (18.0) 659 (20.3) 88 (16.1) 0.42 Cancer 136 (3.8) 22 (4.1) 141 (4.3) 12 (2.2) 0.076 Charlson Score (mean ± SD) 2.6 ± 2.3 2.6 ± 2.4 2.7 ± 2.5 2.3 ± 2.0 0.015 Open in a new tab * CVD includes MI, CHF, cerebrovascular disease; SLE=systemic lupus erythematosus Overall, the demographics remained comparable for the current smoking visits pre- (n=543) and post-intervention (n=550) ( Table 1 ). Only the Charlson comorbidity score (p=0.015) and the rheumatoid arthritis prevalence (p=0.027) were significantly lower among post-intervention visits with current smoking. Notably, the present cohort was 89.8% Black race and had 94.2% on public insurance or uninsured. All elements of the tobacco use screening, assessment for readiness to quit or cut back, and referral to cessation class or quit line increased post-intervention ( Table 2 ). Tobacco use screening increased significantly [87.9% (n = 3370/3833) of post- versus 81.9% (n = 3407/4158) of pre-intervention visits (p<0.001)]. Among visits with tobacco users, readiness to quit was assessed in 31.8% (n = 175/550) of patient-visits post- vs 8.1% (n = 44/543) pre-implementation (p<0.001). Among those whose readiness to quit was assessed, quit readiness reached 58.9% (n = 103/175) post-intervention, an increase of almost 50 percentage points from pre-implementation. Among smokers, 105 were offered referral to the quit-line or cessation class post intervention, compared to six pre-intervention [19.1% (n = 105/550) vs 1.1% (n = 6/543), p<0.001]. Post-intervention, 96 people who smoked accepted a referral to the quit line (n = 96/550=17.5%; ITT 96/103=93.2% among those ready to quit), and 18 accepted referral to a smoking cessation class (n = 18/550=3.3%; ITT 18/103=17.4%), compared to 1.1% (n = 6/543) who had accepted class referral before QC implementation. Table 2. Visit level care steps observed pre and post protocol among visits with tobacco users Pre-Protocol n=4158 % of visits Post-Protocol n=3833 % of visits p Tobacco Status Assessed (All Visits) 3407/4158 (81.9) 3370/3833 (87.9) <0.001 Tobacco User Visits 543/3407 (15.9) 550/3370 (16.3) 0.67 Readiness to Quit Assessed 44/543 (8.1) 175/550 (31.8) <0.001 Ready to Quit 4/44 (9.1) 103/175 (58.9) <0.001 Quit line or Class E-Referral Offered Referral Offered 6/543 (1.1) 105/550 (19.1) <0.001 Quit line E-referral Accepted 0/543 (0) 96/550 (17.5) <0.001 Class E-referral Accepted 6/543 (1.1) 18/550 (3.3) 0.021 Either Accepted 6/543 (1.1) 102/550 (18.5) <0.001 Both Declined 0/543 (0) 4/550 (0.7) 0.12 Open in a new tab Per Table 3 regression models, the odds of tobacco use screening, readiness to quit assessment, and referrals were all significantly increased post- compared to pre-intervention. Even after adjusting for age, sex, race/ethnicity, comorbidity, and payer, the odds of tobacco use screening increased (aOR 1.6 (1.44–1.85)) post-intervention. The odds of the assessment of readiness to quit was five times higher post- compared to pre-intervention (aOR 5.2 (3.59–7.38)). The odds of referral to either quit line or class increased over 20-fold post-intervention (aOR 20.7 (8.96–47.59)). Table 3. Visit level odds of tobacco care steps Unadjusted OR (95% CI) Adjusted OR (95% CI) Pre Protocol Tobacco Use Assessed * Ref Ref Post Protocol Tobacco Use Assessed * 1.60 (1.42–1.82) 1.63 (1.44–1.85) Pre Protocol Ready to Quit Assessed Ref Ref Post Protocol Ready to Quit Assessed 5.29 (3.70–7.56) 5.15