s cost $174.80/102= $1.71 each. With 17 reported quit attempts, this equals $174.80/17 = $10.28/quit attempt. Assuming a published baseline notes 7.5% of smokers quit annually, 40 then the new rate of 17% reporting a quit estimates a number needed to treat (NNT) of 10.5 referrals per additional quit (NNT=1/(Absolute risk reduction is difference between control and intervention)). Staff Perceptions Clinic staff (n = 3) viewed the protocol as feasible and acceptable in interviews and questionnaire items. One medical assistant noted, “A lot more people are responsive than I thought would be.” As another medical assistant observed, “When the patient complains about their pain, I can segue to the smoking information card and tell them that smoking makes their disease worse.” Staff also suggested improvements to the patient education brochure as well as process implementation, such as staff reminders prior to clinic sessions. In questionnaires administered 20 months post-implementation, 100% of staff gave the highest rating on a 5-point Likert scale for the one-on-one audit feedback in three categories: respectful, helpful feedback, and ease of understanding their data through color-coded graphical reports (see Supplemental Table ). DISCUSSION In a safety-net rheumatology clinic serving a majority of patients identifying as Black race, many were interested in quitting smoking when asked and had not been offered support previously in the rheumatology clinic. Within three months, Quit Connect significantly increased referrals to a tobacco quit line and cessation class and was sustained over two years despite the COVID-19 pandemic. Success replicated prior positive findings at two Midwest centers with very different populations and resources (majority White and commercially insured previously; Table 6 ). 34 – 36 Comparatively, this Quit Connect study showed efficacy in a majority Black population with more Medicaid, Medicare, and uninsured, as well as a high proportion of lupus and comorbidities. The intervention process was easily integrated into the EHR, effectively engaged rheumatology staff, and leveraged free, state-funded resources. Data showed effectiveness with 20-fold higher referrals and good staff acceptability, including enthusiasm to participate in the intervention. Monthly or bi-monthly audit feedback reports for individual staff continued to encourage brief intervention for rheumatology patients who smoked. Quarterly group level reports are still being shared to encourage sustainability. Post-intervention, patients preferred quit line referrals over classes. During the pandemic and for a period after the pandemic, classes were suspended, and only quit line referrals remained a viable option. Despite pandemic barriers, many patients were connected directly to the quit line and set a quit date and accepted nicotine replacement. Increasing cessation referrals in this study stands in contrast to national trends where smoking rates increased and quit line use declined nationally during the pandemic. 41 Likewise, cost-effectiveness for the health system in this study was favorable at $4–10 per reported quit attempt, and <$1 per referral, much like our prior study. 35 Our findings support use of the Quit Connect as a scalable public health strategy in other systems, including safety-net or underserved settings. As White, non-Hispanic patients were less likely to accept referrals ( Table 6 ), this may suggest a benefit of racially concordant staff to facilitate additional referrals to smoking cessation resources. 42 , 43 The integration of Quit Connect within the EHR does require some time on the part of the IT team that varies. Streamlining the EHR process to assess readiness to quit using existing EHR functionality, and standardizing e-referral features in the EHR could facilitate even greater screening and more referrals to the quit line or class. Future studies could also investigate further dissemination and implementation strategies for Quit Connect and other cessation support in rheumatology clinics, as well as the potential benefits of implementing with racially concordant staff. 43 This finding could reflect Black staff feeling more comfortable with making cessation offers to Black patients, or higher likelihood of patients to accept referral in racially concordant pairs. A recent Kaiser Family Foundation study noted that 74% of patients of Black race reported “trust most of the time” with racially concordant doctors, versus 65% in nonconcordant pairs. 44 Given that only 5% of rheumatologists and 5% of all doctors in Georgia are of Black race, this also underscores the benefits of diverse staff teams and ongoing DEI efforts. 45 , 46 Limitations Despite strengths of our study, including a large population reach and standardized EHR assessment, we acknowledge limitations. First, this pre-post study does not match the robustness of a randomized trial. In addition, baseline data from abstracted records were not as complete as the post-implementation EHR reports, but similar to our prior published abstracted data in another system. 29 Likewise, IT build and reporting time were covered by grant funding and cost and capability may vary by center. The influence of racial concordance between staff and patients was not rigorously evaluated and does not account for multi-dimensional measures of concordance but is hypothesis generating. 42 Generalizability of equal pre-pandemic preference for the class versus phone support might differ in populations by employment status and travel distance. Likewise, the intervention hiatus caused by the pandemic resulted in a reboot training on the intervention, which appears to have improved fidelity. This suggests benefits of incorporating periodic refresher trainings on the protocol. We acknowledge limits of patients who were unreachable or declined. Many were not reached despite five call attempts, raising interest in updating patient contact details more frequently, improving caller identification information, or other strategies. Some also declined services once reached, 19% vs 8% in our prior study. 35 This could differ by population or pandemic stressors. Lastly, our study was short-term, and given that persons who smoke tobacco may require ten or more attempts before quitting permanently, 47 long-term population outcomes are of interest. CONCLUSIONS Across two years, in a safety-net rheumatology clinic serving predominantly Black patients, 58.9% of visits with smokers were ready to cut back or quit when asked, and 18.5% of those individuals accepted referral to cessation support. Quit Connect was feasible, cost-effective, and sustainable even during the COVID-19 pandemic, and could be considered for future dissemination to help rheumatology patients stop smoking. Given the importance of smoking cessation to reduce CVD and rheumatic disease burden, future studies should disseminate Quit Connect to other health systems to leverage free state quit lines and improve population health. Supplementary Material Supinfo NIHMS1985975-supplement-Supinfo.docx (41.3KB, docx) Significance and Innovations. Quit-Connect , a staff-led smoking cessation intervention that allowed for direct EHR referral to the state quit line, was implemented in this safety-net rheumatology clinic and increased cessation referrals 20-fold (aOR 20.88 [CI 9.05–48.15]). Engaging clinic staff to check (assess) tobacco use, advise patients to quit, and connect patients to tobacco cessation resources leveraged team-based care to deliver a cost-effective intervention ($4–10 per quit attempt). Over two years in this safety-net rheumatology clinic serving predominantly Black patients, 58.9% of visits with smokers indicated readiness to cut back or quit when asked. Post-implementation, among 550 visits with patients who smoked, 102 people accepted a referral to either the quit line or a cessation class (18.5%, n=102/550 patients who smoke; 93% intention to treat, n= 96 of 103 patients ready to quit) while quit line rates were declining nationally. Acknowledgments. The authors would like to acknowledge the cooperation of the IT, nursing and medical staff in the study hospital and would like to thank the Georgia Department of Health and Optum quit line services for their support. Funding: Supported by Independent Grants for Learning and Change (Pfizer) Award ID 44110255 (PI: Bartels) and by a grant collaboration from the University of Wisconsin Clinical and Translational Science Award and School of Medicine and Public Health’s Wisconsin Partnership Program, through the NIH National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (grant UL1TR002373). Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality grant 1K01HS028926 (Ramly). Footnotes Preliminary results of this research were presented as poster abstracts at the American College of Rheumatology Convergence Annual Meeting, November 6, 2020 and November 7, 2021 (virtual). REFERENCES 1. Avina-Zubieta JA, Choi HK, Sadatsafavi M, et al. Risk of cardiovascular mortality in patients with rheumatoid arthritis: a meta-analysis of observational studies. Arthritis Rheum 2008; 59: 1690–1697. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Avina-Zubieta JA, Thomas J, Sadatsafavi M, et al. Risk of incident cardiovascular events in patients with rheumatoid arthritis: a meta-analysis of observational studies. Ann Rheum Dis 2012; 71: 1524–1529. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Lassere MN, Rappo J, Portek IJ, et al. How many life years are lost in patients with rheumatoid arthritis? Secular cause-specific and all-cause mortality in rheumatoid arthritis, and their predictors in a long-term Australian cohort study. Intern Med J 2013; 43: 66–72. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Manzi S, Meilahn EN, Rairie JE, et al. Age-specific incidence rates of myocardial infarction and angina in women with systemic lupus erythematosus: comparison with the Framingham Study. Am J Epidemiol 1997; 145: 40