INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3973,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10500561','Childhood-Onset Lupus Nephritis in the Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance Registry: Short-Term Kidney Status and Variation in Care.','Smitherman EA, Chahine RA, Beukelman T, Lewandowski LB, Rahman AKMF, Wenderfer SE, Curtis JR, Hersh AO; CARRA Registry Investigators.','2023','waitlist antimalarials cdmards belimumab nonproliferative nonprivate sociodemographics 1with mycopheno nonnephritis nihms1927124 supplementary_table_1a__1b__2 niams subinvestigators smitherman gilkeson subinvestigators subinvestigators abulaban agbayani aiello akoghlanian alejandro allenspach alperin alpizar amarilyo ambler ardoin armendariz balboni balevic ballenger ballinger balmuri barbar smiley barillas basiaga baszis brunson beltz benham benseler beukelman bigley binstadt blakley bohnsack boneparth bracaglia bukulmez canna carper cerracchio chalom hoftman chira chinn chundru clairman confair conlon correll corvalan costanzo cron curiel curington dalrymple dedeoglu deguzman delnay dempsey desantis dingle dover driest durkee duverger dvergsten eberhard eckert edelheit edens edens edgerly ervin fadrhonc falcon favier federici fennell ferrucho finkel fogel froese fuhlbrigge gerhold gerstbacher gillispie giverc godiwala goheer gotschlich gotte gracia grevich griswold grom guevara guittar hager halyabar hammelev hance harel haro harry hartigan hausmann heiart hekl henrickson hersh hillyer hiraki hiskey hobday hoffart huggins huttenlocher ibarra imundo inman insalaco janow jaquith jared justiniano karan khalsa kienzle kingsbury kitcharoensakkul klausmeier gitelman kompelien kosikowski kovalick kracker lapidus lapin lasky latham laxer lentini lingis lowman lvovich magni manzoni malla maller malloy mannion manos martyniuk mathus mccurdy mccurdy mchale mcmonagle mcmullen meidan mellins merritt michalowski miettunen milojevic mirizio misajon modica moorthy dewitt moussa mruk muscal nadler nahal nasah nassi nativ natter neely newhall nicolai nigrovic nocton oberle obispo okeke onel orandi osei onomah pagano paller panupattanapong pardeo pentakota pepmueller phillippi pires marafon phillippi ponder pooni prahalad protopapas puplava quach quinlan rabinovich radhakrishna rafko raisian rakestraw randell remmel repp riebschleger ringold riskalla rivas chacon rodela rodriquez ronis rosenkranz rosolowski rothermel wojcicki rouster sabbagh sadun sandborg sanni sarkissian savani scalzi schanberg scharnhorst schikler schlefman schmeling schollaert fitch schulert seay seper shalen shelly shenoi shergill shirley shishov shivers sivaraman sletten smitherman soep spitznagle sran srinivasalu stapp steigerwald sterba rakovchik stingl sule sumner sundel sutter syverson szymanski taber tambralli taneja tapani tarshish tarvin taxter tesher thatayatikom tiffany tipp toib torok toruner tory toth tubwell twilt uriguen valcarcel vannoy vasquez vazzana vehe veiga verbsky vilar scheven vora wahezi waite wampler muskardin waqar waterfield weiser wershba woolnough yalcindag yomogida zapata zartoshti zeft zeft zic downloadsupplement 2facr acr25002 disclosureform gladman hersh trupin hersh scheven shaikhani hanly multistate hiraki smitherman pooni mina scheven ardoin greenler graeff weening agati mangione decristofaro setodji mina gitelman rouster beukelman ilowite orbai gladman urowitz churg glassock igaky shoin gladman ginzler vazzana daga goilav hagelberg bargman longterm gipson massengill boneparth ilowite rianthavorn buddhasri boneparth wenderfer moorthy abadi armon suhlrie hennies gellermann maroz bitencourt makris solow wenderfer shatat bajema wilhelmus alpers goilav nihms1927124 supplementary_table_1a__1b__2','Abstract Objective. The goal was to characterize short-term kidney status and describe variation in early care utilization in a multicenter cohort of patients with childhood-onset systemic lupus erythematosus (cSLE) and nephritis. Methods. We analyzed previously collected prospective data from North American patients with cSLE with kidney biopsy-proven nephritis enrolled in the Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance (CARRA) Registry from March 2017 through December 2019. We determined the proportion of patients with abnormal kidney status at the most recent registry visit and applied generalized linear mixed models to identify associated factors. We also calculated frequency of medication use, both during induction and ever recorded. Results. We identified 222 patients with kidney biopsy–proven nephritis, with 64% class III/IV nephritis on initial biopsy. At the most recent registry visit at median (interquartile range) of 17 (8–29) months from initial kidney biopsy, 58 of 106 patients (55%) with available data had abnormal kidney status. This finding was associated with male sex (odds ratio [OR] 3.88, 95% confidence interval [95% CI] 1.21–12.46) and age at cSLE diagnosis (OR 1.23, 95% CI 1.01–1.49). Patients with class IV nephritis were more likely than class III to receive cyclophosphamide and rituximab during induction. There was substantial variation in mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, and rituximab ever use patterns across rheumatology centers. Conclusion. In this cohort with predominately class III/IV nephritis, male sex and older age at cSLE diagnosis were associated with abnormal short-term kidney status. We also observed substantial variation in contemporary medication use for pediatric lupus nephritis between pediatric rheumatology centers. Additional studies are needed to better understand the impact of this variation on long-term kidney outcomes. INTRODUCTION Long-term outcomes for patients with childhood-onset systemic lupus erythematosus (cSLE) are characterized by increased morbidity ( 1 ) and mortality ( 2 ) compared with adult-onset disease. This is further complicated by an increased incidence of lupus nephritis in cSLE compared with adult-onset ( 3 ) with kidney disease as a leading cause of long-term damage in cSLE ( 4 ). Poorly controlled lupus nephritis can progress to kidney failure, requiring intensive kidney replacement treatments or transplantation that result in a substantial quality of life and economic burden on patients and the health system ( 5 ). In children with lupus nephritis–associated kidney failure in the US, disparities in both care delivery and health outcomes, including transplant waitlist placement, receipt of kidney transplant, and mortality, have been previously observed based on age, race, ethnicity, insurance status, and geographic region ( 6 ). Reducing inappropriate variation in care delivery is an important strategy to minimize health care disparities and improve health outcomes. Evidence-based guidelines can be used to identify key interventions to implement in practice ( 7 ). The 2012 Kidney Disease Improving Global Outcomes Clinical Practice Guideline for lupus nephritis excluded children because of the insufficient availability of evidence ( 8 ), but statements largely based on expert opinion have been since published. Consensus treatment plans for newly diagnosed childhood-onset proliferative lupus nephritis outlined the use of either mycophenolate or cyclophosphamide for induction therapy ( 9 ). In 2013, a set of 26 quality indicator measures for preventive processes of care in cSLE, including nephritis and hypertension management, were developed ( 10 ). The Single Hub and Access Point for Paediatric Rheumatology in Europe recommendations for the diagnosis and treatment of childhood-onset lupus nephritis published in 2017 ( 11 ) outline, using the 2003 International Society of Nephrology (ISN)/Renal Pathology Society (RPS) classification for kidney biopsy pathology ( 12 ), starting an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor or angiotensin-receptor blocker (ARB) for proteinuria and preferred treatments for each pathologic nephritis classification. However, it is also well-established that a substantial time lag exists in implementing best practices across health care, including for pediatric chronic diseases ( 13 ). Although minimal standards of care have been defined, initial benchmarking studies have demonstrated significant gaps in performance on cSLE quality indicator measures ( 14 ) and low uptake of consensus treatment plans ( 15 ). Because of the time since publication and the small sample size of these initial studies, respectively, there remains limited contemporary, real-world data on variability in the patterns of care utilization for the initial management of childhood-onset lupus nephritis. More importantly, the impact of early care indicators on long-term kidney outcomes remains largely unknown, making the identification of effective care interventions likely to reduce outcome disparities a challenge. As an initial step, the objectives of this study were to 1) characterize short-term kidney status in a modern cohort of children with recent-onset lupus nephritis and 2) identify variability in the patterns of early care utilization including kidney biopsy and medications. We hypothesized that differences in short-term kidney status would be associated with self-reported race and ethnicity and that differences in medication use would be observed across pediatric rheumatology centers and by nephritis class. PATIENTS AND METHODS Patient cohort. This study was conducted as a retrospective analysis of previously collected longitudinal, observational data from the Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance (CARRA) Registry ( 16 ). The CARRA Registry provides the infrastructure for longitudinal data collection across pediatric rheumatic diseases, including cSLE-related conditions. The CARRA cSLE Registry began enrolling patients in March 2017 with the aim to capture incident or recent-onset cSLE cases through convenience sampling at pediatric rheumatology centers in North America. The goal for including recent-onset cases was to minimize selection bias against enrolling patients with more complex disease at diagnosis but still allow for completeness and accuracy of historical data at time of enrollment. Patients eligible for registry enrollment include children and adolescents age <18 years old at cSLE diagnosis and <21 years old at time of enrollment. The Systemic Lupus International Collaborating Clinics (SLICC) classification criteria ( 17 ) is used, defining probable SLE as meeting 3 criteria and definite SLE as meeting 4 criteria. The guideline for enrollment is within 24 months of the date of cSLE diagnosis or within 24 months of new-onset lupus nephritis. We included patients enrolled in the CARRA cSLE Registry from March 2017 through December 2019 for this study. Data source. Data in the CARRA Registry represents real-world data for the sample of enrolled patients. Data is collected within the context of routine clinical care through both medical record extraction and clinician completion of case report forms and is then manually entered into a web-based database. After enrollment, follow-up data are collected every 6 (±3) months, aligned with clinical visits. At the time of enrollment, medication data are captured for the entire interval of time since disease onset and is updated at each subsequent registry visit. Visit-level data, such as SLE Disease Activity Index 2000 ( 18 ), are only collected from enrollment forward and is therefore unavailable prior to enrollment. The CARRA Registry is approved by the Duke University Institutional Review Board, and each participating site has obtained local internal review board approval. Definition of lupus nephritis. For this study, kidney biopsy records were required for inclusion because this was the most reliable method to identify presence of nephritis in the registry. Lupus nephritis was defined using either the 1995 World Health Organization (WHO) ( 19 ) or 2003 ISN/RPS classification systems ( 12 ). A subset of patients had classification recorded using both WHO and ISN/RPS; 1 patient with discrepant classification (WHO class V versus ISN/RPS class III) was removed from analysis. Three additional patients with positive histopathologic results but no kidney biopsy date recorded were excluded from analysis. We included all patients with lupus nephritis, including class I, in our cohort. Patients with class III/IV or mixed class III+V/IV+V were categorized as proliferative nephritis. Patients with class V only were categorized as pure membranous nephritis. Measures. We extracted the following patient-level variables from the registry: self-reported sex, self-reported race and ethnicity (from a fixed set of categories with race and ethnicity combined), insurance status at enrollment (collected as private, Medicaid, state health plan, Medicare, military, non-US insurance for patients in Canada, other, none), self-reported household income, self-reported parental education level, and age at cSLE diagnosis. We also extracted the following visit-level variables from the registry: date of cSLE diagnosis, date of initial kidney biopsy, date of repeat kidney biopsies if applicable, WHO or ISN/RPS classification of initial kidney biopsy, classification of repeat kidney biopsies if applicable, cumulative number of both ACR ( 20 ) and SLICC classification criteria ( 17 ), SLICC damage index ( 21 ) at most recent registry visit, and recorded laboratory and urinalysis data at most recent visit. Finally, we extracted all medications recorded at any time point. cSLE-related medications were categorized as antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine); conventional disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (cDMARDs), including mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, azathioprine, methotrexate, tacrolimus, and leflunomide; and biologic DMARDs including rituximab, intravenous immunoglobulin, belimumab, and abatacept. We also collected available data on ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and diuretics. Because of substantial missing data in recorded urinalysis and laboratory data as previously reported ( 22 ), we adapted existing definitions for complete and partial response ( 11 ) and defined short-term kidney status using 1) estimated glomerular filtration ratio (eGFR), calculated from the most recent serum creatinine value using the bedside Schwartz equation ( 23 ), and 2) proteinuria, measured by the most recent urine protein to creatinine ratio (uPCR) or urine dipstick results if the uPCR was unavailable. We defined normal kidney status as eGFR ≥90 ml/minute/1.73m 2 plus uPCR ≤0.5 mg/mg or no protein on urine dipstick. We defined abnormal kidney status as eGFR <90 ml/minute/1.73m 2 or uPCR >0.5 mg/mg or 1+ or greater protein on urine dipstick. To understand patterns of medication use, a hierarchical categorization schema was applied so that patients categorized as receiving cyclophosphamide and/or rituximab may have also received mycophenolate or other DMARDs but not vice versa. Patients with both mycophenolate and other DMARD use recorded were categorized as receiving mycophenolate. We identified medications used for lupus nephritis induction therapy based on recorded medication start and stop dates compared with the date of initial positive kidney biopsy: the induction period was defined as 30 days before or 60 days after the date of kidney biopsy. We selected this definition to 1) account for medications started before kidney biopsy and continued, and 2) to provide a liberal follow-up period for medications to be started after kidney biopsy. Medications with start dates within this period were considered induction medications. Statistical analysis. Patient characteristics including sociodemographic and clinical characteristics at registry enrollment were summarized across all patients with kidney biopsy–proven nephritis. Kidney biopsy timing and pathologic classification were also summarized for all patients in the cohort. Frequency distributions were used for categorical variables, and mean with SD or median with interquartile range (IQR) were used for continuous and count variables. Bivariate analysis was conducted using chi-square and Fisher’s exact tests (when appropriate) for categorical variables and Wilcoxon’s test for continuous and count variables. Multivariable logistic regression analysis was performed by employing generalized linear mixed models with logit link and centers as random effect to assess risk factors for abnormal short-term kidney status at the most recent registry visit. The statistical model included nephritis classification (class III/III+V, class IV/IV+V, or nonproliferative nephritis) and time since initial kidney biopsy. We included centers as a random intercept to account for differences between centers and possible correlated outcomes from the same site because of the clustering nature of data. We also considered all the covariates from Supplementary Table 1b with P < 0.05 (available on the Arthritis Care & Research website at http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/acr.25002 ) and performed variable selection methods to identify covariates for the final multivariable model. The frequencies of recorded cSLE-related medications were calculated both as ever use and within induction period. Medication frequencies were calculated across the total nephritis cohort, for patients with proliferative nephritis, and for patients with membranous nephritis. To evaluate the association between patient-level sociodemographic and clinical characteristics and induction therapy agent (cyclophosphamide versus mycophenolate; rituximab versus no rituximab), we performed bivariate analysis as described above. All performed tests were 2-sided with a 0.05 level of significance, and all analyses were conducted using SAS, version 9.4. RESULTS Patient characteristics. Out of 565 patients with definite or probable cSLE enrolled in the CARRA Registry between March 2017 and December 2019, we identified 222 (39%) patients with kidney biopsy–proven lupus nephritis. The cohort was 83% female, 31% Black, 25% Hispanic, 22% White, 15% Asian, and 7% other ( Table 1 ). A majority of patients (59%) had nonprivate insurance, including Medicaid, Medicare, state plan, military, non-US, or other. Only 6 patients reported no insurance. There was a wide range of reported household income and parent education level, including 40% reporting annual household income less than $75,000 per year and 35% reporting parental completion of high school or less. Table 1. Sociodemographic and clinical characteristics of patients with kidney biopsy-proven lupus nephritis in the CARRA SLE Registry * Patient characteristics Value (n = 222) Sociodemographics Female, n (%) 184 (83) Race and ethnicity, n (%) Black, African American, African, or Afro-Caribbean 69 (31) Hispanic, Latino, or Spanish origin 55 (25) White 49 (22) Asian 29 (15) Other 20 (7) Insurance status, n (%) Public (Medicaid, state health plan, Medicare, military) 96 (44) Private 92 (41) Other (non-US, other, none) † 34 (15) Household income, n (%) <$25,000 37 (17) $25,000–74,999 51 (23) ≥$75,000 91 (41) Unknown/prefer not to answer 43 (19) Parent education level, n (%) High school or less 72 (35) At least some college 100 (49) Unknown/prefer not to answer 34 (17) Clinical characteristics Age at cSLE diagnosis, mean ± SD years, (min-max) 13.6 ± 3.0 (0.7–18.4) Time between symptom onset and cSLE diagnosis, months, median (IQR) 2.0 (0.7–4.6) Time between cSLE diagnosis and registry enrollment, months, median (IQR) 7.5 (2.2–18.3) Cumulative number of ACR classification criteria, median (IQR) 5 (4–6) Cumulative number of SLICC classification criteria, median (IQR) 9 (7–11) Open in a new tab * CARRA = Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance; cSLE = childhood-onset systemic lupus erythematosus; IQR = interquartile range; max = maximum; min = minimum; SLE = systemic lupus erythematosus; SLICC = Systemic Lupus International Collaborating Clinics. † Please note that non-US insurance refers to patients located and insured in Canada. The mean ± SD age at cSLE diagnosis was 13.6 ± 3.0 years, with median (IQR) time from reported symptom onset to diagnosis of 2.0 (0.7–4.6) months. Year of cSLE diagnosis ranged from 2007 to 2019, although 90% of patients were diagnosed in 2015 or later. The median (IQR) time from cSLE diagnosis to enrollment was 7.5 (2.2–18.3) months. At time of analysis, patients had an average of 3 registry visits recorded and a maximum of 6 visits. Kidney biopsy timing and pathologic classification. The median (IQR) time between initial kidney biopsy and date of cSLE diagnosis was 0 (−2.0–0.5) months ( Table 2 ). Only 14% of patients had kidney biopsy occur more than 1 year following cSLE diagnosis, with a maximum duration of 7 years. Time between date of kidney biopsy and registry enrollment was median (IQR) of 5.4 (1.4–13.2) months, and only 14 (6%) initial kidney biopsies occurred following registry enrollment. Time between first abnormal urinalysis or laboratory values and kidney biopsy was not available. Table 2. Kidney biopsy timing and pathologic classification for patients with lupus nephritis in the CARRA Registry * Kidney biopsy timing and pathologic classification Value (n = 222) Time between initial kidney biopsy and cSLE diagnosis, months, median (IQR) † 0 (−2.0–0.5) Time between initial kidney biopsy and registry enrollment, months, median (IQR) † 5.4 (1.4–13.2) Time between date of kidney biopsy and most recent registry visit, months, median (IQR) 17.3 (8.0–29.0) Pathologic class per 1995 WHO system only, n (%) 120 (54) Pathologic class per 2003 ISN/RPS system only, n (%) 64 (29) Pathologic class per both 1995 WHO and 2003 ISN/RPS systems, n (%) 38 (17) Pathologic class of initial kidney biopsy, n (%) Class I/II 36 (16) Class III 63 (28) Class IV 79 (36) Class V 29 (13) Mixed class III+V/IV+V 14 (6) Class VI 1 (1) More than one kidney biopsy recorded, n (%) 19 (9) Open in a new tab * CARRA = Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance; cSLE = childhood-onset systemic lupus erythematosus; IQR = interquartile range; ISN = International Society of Nephrology; RPS = Renal Pathology Society; WHO = World Health Organization. † Kidney biopsy occurring before cSLE diagnosis or registry enrollment were calculated as positive values. Kidney biopsy occurring after date of cSLE diagnosis or registry enrollment were calculated as negative values. On initial kidney biopsy, most patients (64%) had class III/IV proliferative nephritis ( Table 2 ). Histopathology was classified using 1995 WHO in 54%, 2003 ISN/RPS in 29%, and both in 17% of patients. More than one kidney biopsy was recorded for 19 patients (9%) with change in class recorded in 68% of repeat biopsies. Reason for repeat biopsy was not collected. Initial biopsy results were used for analysis. Short-term kidney status. At the most recent registry visit at a median (IQR) of 17.3 (8.0–29.0) months from initial kidney biopsy, the mean ± SD SLICC damage index was 0.4 ± 1.1with a range of 0 to 8. eGFR was calculated from the creatinine value recorded at the most recent visit for 158 patients (71% of total). Of these, 111 (70%) patients had eGFR ≥90 ml/minute/1.73m 2 , 32 (20%) had 60 to 90 ml/minute/1.73m 2 , and 15 (10%) had <60 ml/minute/1.73m 2 . Data were available for 107 patients (48% of total) to calculate short-term kidney status at the most recent registry visit, and 59 (55%) had abnormal status. Sex and age at diagnosis in years were significantly different between patients with and without kidney status data, but there were no differences in other clinical variables ( Supplementary Table 1a , available on the Arthritis Care & Research website at http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/acr.25002 ). Sex and age at diagnosis in years were significantly different between patients with abnormal versus normal kidney status ( P = 0.006 and 0.012, respectively), whereas race and ethnicity was not statistically different between the 2 groups ( Supplementary Table 1b , available at http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/acr.25002 ). Adjusting for pathologic class, disease duration, and centers, we found male sex (adjusted odds ratio [aOR] 3.88, 95% confidence interval [95% CI] 1.21–12.46) and age at diagnosis in years (aOR 1.23, 95%CI 1.01–1.49) remained independently associated with abnormal kidney status ( Supplementary Table 2 , available on the Arthritis Care & Research website at http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/acr.25002 ). Medication utilization: induction period. New medications started during the induction period of 30 days before and 60 days after the date of initial kidney biopsy were recorded in 83% of the total nephritis cohort, 85% of the proliferative nephritis group, and 87% of the pure membranous nephritis group ( Table 3 ). Of the 38 patients with no new medications started during the defined induction period, only 9 had class I/II nephritis. A total of 10 patients had medications started before the date of kidney biopsy with no updates recorded, and 28 had medication start dates recorded from 60 to 365 days after the date of initial kidney biopsy, at a mean of 180 days. The pattern of no induction medication entries appeared to be at random because there were no center-level differences and there was no difference in average number of registry visits for patients with medication data recorded in the induction period interval compared to those out of the interval. Table 3. New medications recorded during the nephritis induction period, defined as 30 days before and 60 days following the date of initial kidney biopsy, in the CARRA Registry * Medication category, n (%) Total nephritis cohort (n = 222) Proliferative nephritis (n = 156) † Pure membranous nephritis (n = 29) ‡ Mycophenolate 89 (40) 65 (42) 14 (48) Cyclophosphamide 26 (12) 25 (16) 1 (3) Cyclophosphamide + rituximab 19 (8) 15 (10) 2 (7) Rituximab 15 (7) 10 (6) 2 (7) Hydroxychloroquine only 16 (7) 11 (7) 1 (3) Other DMARD 11 (5) 2 (1) 3 (10) Glucocorticoids without DMARD 8 (4) 4 (3) 1 (3) No new medications recorded § 38 (17) 24 (15) 5 (17) Open in a new tab * Values are the number (%). CARRA = Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance; DMARD = disease-modifying antirheumatic drug. † Proliferative defined as class III/IV or mixed class III+V/IV+V. ‡ Pure membranous defined as class V. § Of the patients with no new medications recorded during the induction period, 10 had new medications recorded before the defined period, and 28 had new medications recorded following the defined period. Across the total cohort, the predominant DMARD used was mycophenolate, followed by cyclophosphamide including rituximab combination, and least rituximab without cyclophosphamide ( Table 3 ). Higher cyclophosphamide use was noted in patients with proliferative compared with pure membranous nephritis, and higher other DMARD use was noted in pure membranous compared with proliferative nephritis. Some patients had only hydroxychloroquine recorded during the induction period, and fewer patients had only glucocorticoids recorded, either intravenous or oral. For patients in the proliferative nephritis group, there were no differences in age at diagnosis, sex, race and ethnicity, insurance status, reported household income, reported parent education level, cumulative number of ACR criteria, or mixed pathologic class between patients who received cyclophosphamide compared with mycophenolate only during the induction period. However, patients with class IV nephritis were more likely than patients with class III nephritis to receive cyclophosphamide (62% versus 9%) and less likely to receive mycopheno-late only (38% versus 91%) ( P < 0.001), during the induction period. We conducted the same comparison between patients with proliferative nephritis who received any rituximab or no rituximab during the induction period. Similarly, the only significant difference between the 2 groups was that patients with class IV nephritis were more likely than patients with class III nephritis to receive rituximab (28% versus 13%) ( P = 0.040). Medication utilization: ever use. We first analyzed all medications recorded through December 2019 for each patient. Ever use of hydroxychloroquine was 97% and mycophenolate was 84% across all biopsy-proven lupus nephritis. Use of other conventional and biologic DMARDs was lower ( Figure 1 ). ACE inhibitor or ARB ever use was recorded for 27% of patients. Diuretic ever use was recorded in 8 patients, 5 with proliferative nephritis and 3 with pure membranous nephritis. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Recorded medication ever use across the total cohort of biopsy-proven lupus nephritis patients in the Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance (CARRA) Registry (n = 222). ACE-I = angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor; ARB = angiotensin-receptor blocker; IV = intravenous. We then examined ever use of mycophenolate, cyclophosphamide, rituximab, cyclophosphamide-rituximab combination, and other DMARDs across 15 centers with ≥5 patients with proliferative nephritis enrolled in the registry. We observed significant variation in medication patterns between centers ( Figure 2 ). Two centers ( Figure 2 , sites A and B) recorded exclusive mycophenolate ever use, and 4 centers ( Figure 2 , sites C–F) had predominant mycophenolate or other DMARD with low cyclophosphamide use. There was variable but increasing use of cyclophosphamide and rituximab across 7 centers ( Figure 2 , sites G–M), including use of cyclophosphamide-rituximab in combination. Finally, 2 centers ( Figure 2 , sites N and O) only recorded cyclophosphamide and rituximab use for all patients, including 1 center only recording cyclophosphamide-rituximab in combination. Medication indication for treatment of nephritis or nonnephritis manifestations was not collected. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Center-level variation in ever use of mycophenolate only, cyclophosphamide, rituximab, cyclophosphamide-rituximab combination, or other disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs (DMARDs) for patients with biopsy-proven proliferative nephritis. Includes 15 pediatric rheumatology centers, Sites A–O, with ≥5 patients enrolled in the Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance (CARRA) Registry with proliferative nephritis. A hierarchical categorization schema was applied so that patients categorized as cyclophosphamide and/or rituximab may have also ever received mycophenolate or other DMARDs but not vice versa. Patients with both mycophenolate and other DMARD ever use recorded were categorized as mycophenolate. Patients included in the other DMARD category had recorded ever use of azathioprine, methotrexate, or hydroxychloroquine only. DISCUSSION This study is one of the first to analyze the cohort of patients with lupus nephritis enrolled in the new CARRA Registry and one of the largest multicenter registry studies of pediatric lupus nephritis published to date ( 24 – 30 ). Patients enrolled in the CARRA Registry represent a modern cohort of children with lupus nephritis with 90% diagnosed in 2015 or later. Around the time of data collection through December 2019, about 30% of estimated total North American pediatric rheumatology centers had enrolled at least 1 patient. Based on single-center estimates that 25% of patients actively followed are enrolled in the registry, this cohort of 222 pediatric patients with biopsy-proven lupus nephritis may represent 8% of the patient population in North America. Patients with lupus enrolled in the CARRA Registry reported diverse sociodemographic characteristics. Initial kidney biopsy was performed within 30 days of cSLE diagnosis for a majority of patients, and pathologic classification was predominantly proliferative nephritis. We identified differences in short-term kidney status based on sex and age at cSLE diagnosis. We also observed substantial variation in medication use both across nephritis classification and across centers that did not appear to be related to self-reported sex, race and ethnicity, household income, parental education, or insurance status. We used available serum creatinine values to calculate eGFR and available proteinuria values measured by either uPCR or urine dipstick to assess short-term kidney status. Using this modified response definition, we found an association of male sex with abnormal kidney status, a finding that has been well-described in adults with lupus nephritis across multiple adverse nephritis outcomes ( 31 ). We also found cSLE diagnosis at higher age is associated with increasing rate of abnormal kidney status with 23% increase in odds per year of age, although the 95% CI indicated this could be as low as 1% increase for 1-year higher age. Previously, age >16 years at initiation of kidney replacement therapy was associated with decreased kidney transplantation frequency ( 6 ). However, the relationship between age at cSLE diagnosis and kidney outcomes has not been well studied. This is a key consideration for future work because of the increased risk for gaps in care associated with transfer from pediatric to adult health care settings ( 32 ). Data entered into the CARRA Registry represents real-world data for lupus nephritis, which allows for comparison to existing consensus-based treatment recommendations and quality measures. We observed that only 46% of patients had kidney biopsy classified according to the ISN/RPS 2003 system, discordant with the Single Hub and Access Point for Paediatric Rheumatology in Europe recommendations ( 11 ). This low uptake is consistent with a 2014 survey of pediatric rheumatologists and pediatric nephrologists, reporting only 22.4% of institutions surveyed used the 2003 ISN/RPS classification ( 33 ). The more recently updated 2018 ISN/RPS classification system may promote additional uptake because these recommendations aimed to clarify previously problematic definitions ( 34 ). In line with general medication recommendations for cSLE, we observed almost universal use of antimalarial therapy. We also observed predominant use of mycophenolate and cyclophosphamide in proliferative nephritis and mycophenolate in membranous nephritis during the induction period. However, we observed more variation in treatment than would be expected, especially in patients with proliferative nephritis across centers with a range from only mycophenolate use to only cyclophosphamide-rituximab use in combination. We also observed rituximab ever use for 25% of the cohort and during induction for 15% of the cohort, although recommendations are to use it in refractory disease. Although we were unable to evaluate refractory disease, rituximab use was high even within 60 days of kidney biopsy, and there were distinct patterns of rituximab use between centers, suggesting provider preference is likely a strong predictor of rituximab use. This is further supported by results of a survey demonstrating pediatric rheumatologists were significantly more likely to recommend rituximab than pediatric nephrologists ( 35 ). All participating centers are located in North America, although individual site locations are anonymized, so we were unable to further examine regional differences or other center characteristics. We also noted some use of an ACE inhibitor or ARBs, although we were unable to compare the degree of proteinuria at medication start. These medications may be more likely to be prescribed by nephrologists and may be underreported in this registry. Finally, of note, 13% of patients had new medications recorded more than 60 days after the date of initial kidney biopsy, at an average of 180 days, although we are unable to assess if this represents a true gap in care or a gap in data entry. Additional studies of other real-world data sources will be critical to further investigate gaps in care for patients with cSLE nephritis and identify targets for intervention. The primary limitation of this study was missing urinalysis and laboratory data to compare kidney status with induction medication choices. One reason for this limitation is a large eligibility window that promotes patient enrollment but limits the amount of detailed information entered for time points before enrollment. Therefore, we could not include medication choice in our analysis of follow-up kidney status because we were unable to adjust for kidney status at treatment initiation. However, for patients with proliferative nephritis, induction medication choice did not differ by sex or age at diagnosis, so the addition of induction treatment to our short-term kidney status analysis would likely not change our results. Additionally, we were limited in our ability to calculate short-term kidney status and had results for only 48% of the total cohort of patients with nephritis. When we compared patients with and without kidney status data, the only noted differences were higher proportion of males and older age at diagnosis in the group with data. However, in the context of a small sample size, it is unlikely additional kidney status data would change the statistical association found, although it could decrease the magnitude of the odds ratio or narrow the confidence interval. Moreover, the data in this predominately clinician-reported registry is manually entered and susceptible to errors. Because this study was one of the first analyses using this data source, additional efforts are underway to improve the entry of identified missing data. Other considerations for improving data completeness in the future is linking registry data to other real-world data sources. Although cumbersome, especially for a multicenter infrastructure, one of the benefits of electronic health record data is availability of urinalysis and laboratory results and accurate medication prescription dates. Furthermore, administrative claims data can also extend the capability of clinical registry data by providing precise dates for key events and medication fills. A careful plan to identify key time points to enhance completeness of urinalysis results and ability to assess kidney status will be critical for future comparative effectiveness research studies using the CARRA Registry. However, because the CARRA Registry attempts to follow each patient for a minimum of 10 years following enrollment, pertinent long-term outcomes, including the need for kidney replacement treatments or transplantation, is being collected and will be available to analyze in the future. In summary, this initial analysis of the CARRA Registry summarizes pertinent clinical data for one of the largest published cohorts of pediatric patients with lupus nephritis to date. We found that male sex and older age at cSLE diagnosis were associated with abnormal short-term kidney status, suggesting these patients may require closer monitoring, especially considering the complexity of care for transition-age patients. We identified notable variation in kidney biopsy classification system use, patterns of induction therapy, and medication ever use. Future studies to better understand the barriers to recommendation uptake will help identify strategies to reduce this variation in care. Additionally, the generation of stronger evidence of best treatments for childhood-onset lupus nephritis through clinical trials and comparative effectiveness research is needed. Enhancing the ability to assess kidney status in the registry would allow for future comparative effectiveness studies. Determining the key gaps in care that drive disparities in pediatric lupus nephritis is critical for the development of effective interventions to improve outcomes. Supplementary Material Supplementary Table 1a, 1b, 2 NIHMS1927124-supplement-Supplementary_Table_1a__1b__2.docx (36.1KB, docx) SIGNIFICANCE & INNOVATIONS. A modified definition of short-term kidney status at most recent registry visit demonstrated previously established association of male sex with adverse outcomes. Interestingly, older age at lupus diagnosis was also associated with abnormal kidney status. In a contemporary multicenter registry of pediatric patients with lupus nephritis, there was substantial variation observed in medication use patterns, most notable across centers for patients with proliferative nephritis with a range from only mycophenolate use to only cyclophosphamide-rituximab use in combination. While mycophenolate and cyclophosphamide were the most frequently used medications, there was also a high frequency of rituximab use. Rituximab use was common within 60 days of kidney biopsy, and there were distinct patterns of rituximab use between centers, suggesting provider preference is likely a strong predictor of rituximab use. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS This work could not have been accomplished without the aid of the following organizations: The NIH’s National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases (NIAMS) and the Arthritis Foundation. We would also like to thank all participants and hospital sites that recruited patients for the CARRA Registry. We also thank the CARRA Registry site principal investigators, subinvestigators, and research coordinators who are listed in Appendix A . Supported by the Childhood Arthritis and Rheumatology Research Alliance and the Arthritis Foundation. Dr. Smitherman’s work was supported by the Lupus Foundation of America Gary S. Gilkeson Career Development Award and the Rheumatology Research Foundation Investigator Award. APPENDIX A: CARRA REGISTRY SITE PRINCIPAL INVESTIGATORS, SUBINVESTIGATORS, AND RESEARCH COORDINATORS The CARRA Registry site principal investigators, subinvestigators, and research coordinators are as follows: N. Abel, K. Abulaban, A. Adams, M. Adams, R. Agbayani, J. Aiello, S. Akoghlanian, C. Alejandro, E. Allenspach, R. Alperin, M. Alpizar, G. Amarilyo, W. Ambler, E. Anderson, S. Ardoin, S. Armendariz, E. Baker, I. Balboni, S. Balevic, L. Ballenger, S. 