INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4028,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8168457','Spillover Effects From A Consumer-Based Intervention To Increase High-Value Preventive Care.','Cliff BQ, Hirth RA, Mark Fendrick A.','2019','spillovers nudging inducement dependents bargaining enrollee deductibles premium dependents retirees truven marketscan dihydroxyvitamin ekgs indiscriminately clearinghouse indiscriminate uspreventiveservicestaskforce choosingwisely charlesworth meath colla sequist schpero morden lowrisk probit meaned meaned meaned steeper decompose nudge magnitudes complementarity truven marketscan truven marketscan truven marketscan ppts ppts magnitudes ols probit ppts ppts generosity directionally colonoscopies wane promulgate deterrents rebuke discourages nihms1693645 supplementary_material betsy cliff hirth fendrick chernew fendrick healthaffairs fedewa doubeni eskew klink choudhry milstein chernew juster wegh sokol isenberg fendrick fendrick landon elshaug chernew mcwilliams colla morden sequist schpero charlesworth meath rabideau mafi bortz dachary hazel fendrick cassel morden colla sequist qaseem hirth cliff mckellar fendrick agiro devries colla sequist schpero morden probit dimick newhouse keeler leibowitz kaestner sasso heineke geissler brot handel kolstad rabideau nihms1693645 supplementary_material','Abstract Increasing utilization of high-value medical services and reducing services with little or no clinical value are key goals for efficient health systems. Yet, encouraging high-value services may unintentionally impact low-value services. We examine the likelihood of high- and low-value service use after an insurance benefit change in 2011 for one state’s employees that promoted utilization of high-value preventive services. Compared to a control group with stable benefit plans, likelihood of high-value service use increased 11.0 percentage points and likelihood of low-value service use increased 7.9 percentage points in year one. For that year, we associated 74% of the increase in high-value services and 57% of the increase in low-value services with greater use of preventive visits. Our results imply interventions aimed at increasing receipt of high-value preventive services can cause spillovers to low-value services, potentially undermining efficiency gains from the promotion of high-value service use. Introduction Increasing utilization of evidence-based high-value preventive care and discouraging services with few clinical benefits is a major focus of quality improvement in the U.S. health system. Benefit designs and other consumer-facing initiatives such as direct primary care promote high-value care by explicitly nudging consumers toward preventive services through reduced financial barriers and increased access.( 1 )( 2 )( 3 ) A popular and growing benefit structure that emphasizes consumer engagement in this way is value-based insurance design (V-BID), which lowers financial barriers for high-value medical services and has shown success in encouraging consumers to use such services.( 4 )( 5 )( 6 ) Yet, as these efforts promote use of high-value care, they could unintentionally increase use of other services. Medical care is often delivered as a bundle of services, such as a several lab tests that accompany a preventive visit, and inducing use of a service could affect concurrently delivered services. Of particular concern is health care with no or few clinical benefits to patients, often known as low-value care. In recent years, a number of initiatives, including the Choosing Wisely campaign, have attempted to define low-value care and discourage its use. Evidence in recent years has shown the use of low-value care is substantial in both Medicare and the commercially insured.( 7 )( 8 )( 9 )( 10 ) Not only does this care lead to inefficient medical spending, it can sometimes cause patient harm. ( 11 )( 12 )( 13 )( 14 ) To date, we know of no studies that examine the impact of consumer-focused efforts explicitly implemented to increase uptake of high-value care on low-value service use. Accordingly, we assess changes in low-value service use that resulted from such an intervention. Specifically, we focus on low-value preventive services, such as population-wide screenings without evidence of benefit, and on low-value tests or screenings done in outpatient settings as a result of symptomatic concerns. A better understanding of the extent that successful inducement of high-value care introduces unwanted low-value services could better inform future interventions. Program Intervention We studied a benefit redesign program that began in July 2011 and was available to employees in a single US state and their dependents. Details of the bargaining process that led to its adoption as well as additional plan details have been published elsewhere. ( 15 ) Briefly, to address underuse of evidence-based preventive services and chronic disease care, the program incorporates V-BID principles by reducing or eliminating consumer cost sharing for targeted services including preventive office visits, preventive screenings (e.g. cancer screenings), and medications used for management of chronic disease. In general, these targeted services followed guidelines from the U.S. Preventive Service Task Force (USPSTF) at the time of program implementation.( 16 ) A novel feature of the program is a requirement that members receive targeted services. The full list of requirements for the population is in Appendix Exhibit 1 .( 17 ) Enrollee compliance with these requirements is evaluated annually. Enrollees can be removed from the plan for non-compliance, and be switched into an alternative plan without the program’s requirements but with higher deductibles and an additional premium contribution of $100 per month. Enrollment in the the program is voluntary. Ninety-eight percent of employees enrolled in the first year and 98% of those remained in compliance in the program’s first two years. As has been previously shown, adoption of the program led to an increase in utilization of targeted preventive services.( 15 ) Data We used administrative claims data from the state’s employees and their dependents from one plan year before the intervention (July 2010-June 2011) and two years post-intervention (July 2011-June 2013). The data include service dates and claims-level procedure and diagnostic codes for medical services. From an initial cohort of approximately 190,000 enrollees we excluded those who were not continuously enrolled, those with negative annual payment amounts, and those not eligible for the intervention (children and retirees), and those who may have been eligible for Medicare during our study period (older than 61 in 2010). Our final cohort includes 64,165 individuals. We compared outcomes in this group to a control group of continuously enrolled state employees from six Eastern states over the same time period with stable benefit designs from the Truven MarketScan database, with the same exclusion criteria(n=215,314). A full explanation of the cohort creation for the the program population and control sample is in Appendix Exhibit 2 ( 17 ). After exclusions, we used all state health plan enrollees regardless of participation in the program, and refer to this group as the intervention population. We did this for several reasons. First, we are interested in the population-wide effect of the program on low-value service use. Second, as a practical matter, it is impossible to identify those members of the control group who would not have participated in the program if it had been offered in their plan. While these inclusions mean that we interpret our results as intent-to-treat estimates, in practice, as more than 95% of the population remained in the program after two years, these statistics are likely similar in magnitude to an average treatment on the treated effect. The University of Michigan Institutional Review Board determined this study was exempt from review. Outcome Measures Our primary dependent variables are the percentage of people who use any high- or low-value service in the first and second years after the implementation of the program. High-value services include the four preventive disease screenings required at regular intervals to remain compliant in the program (colon cancer screening for individuals over 50, cervical cancer screening for women older than 21, mammography for women beginning at 35, lipid screening at age-specific intervals). These screenings approximate, though deviate slightly from, USPSTF recommendations. Nevertheless, we define them as high-value in this case because they are quite close to recommendations, and because the the program intervention defined them as such. For our set of low-value services we define a set of 13 low-value services using 2011 grade D recommendations from the USPSTF, guidelines from the Choosing Wisely initiative, and previous literature ( Exhibit 1 ).( 16 )( 18 )( 19 ) We chose services commonly delivered in a primary care setting or as a direct referral from it. We include four common preventive screenings for asymptomatic individuals: vitamin D screening, prostate-specific antigen (PSA) screening, cervical cancer screening in women under 21, and cardiac screening in low-risk, asymptomatic individuals. We additionally include nine low-value services done in response to a symptomatic concern, primarily imaging studies. For each of these low-value services we use evidence-based coding methods with procedure and diagnostic codes to exclude cases in which the service are deemed clinically appropriate. ( 9 )( 18 )( 19 ) (For technical coding details, see Appendix Exhibit 3 ).( 17 ) Exhibit 1: Low-Value Service Measures and Rationale SERVICE Source and Recommendation Vitamin D Screen Choosing Wisely “Don’t routinely measure 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D unless the patient has hypercalcemia or decreased kidney function.” Prostate-specific antigen screen † USPSTF; “The USPSTF recommends against prostate-specific antigen-based screening for prostate cancer Cervical Cancer Screen in women under 21 USPSTF “The USPSTF recommends against screening for cervical cancer in women younger than age 21 years” Cardiac screening in asymptomatic individuals USPSTF, Choosing Wisely, American College of Cardiology; “Don’t order annual electrocardiograms (EKGs) or any other cardiac screening for low-risk patients without symptoms.” Imaging for uncomplicated headache Choosing Wisely, American College of Radiology “Don’t do imaging for uncomplicated headache.” Imaging for uncomplicated low back pain Choosing Wisely, American College of Physicians; “Don’t obtain imaging studies in patients with nonspecific low back pain” Electroencephalogram for Headaches Choosing Wisely, American Academy of Neurology “Don’t perform electroencephalography (EEG) for headaches.” CT scan for acute uncomplicated rhinosinusitis Choosing Wisely, American Academy of Allergy, Asthma and Immunology; “Don’t order sinus computed tomography (CT) or indiscriminately prescribe antibiotics for uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis”. Imaging for plantar fasciitis Choosing Wisely, American College of Occupational and Environmental Medicine, “X-ray is not recommended for routine evaluations for plantar fasciitis.” Head imaging for syncope Choosing Wisely, American College of Physicians. “In the evaluation of simple syncope and a normal neurological examination, don’t obtain brain imaging studies (CT or MRI).” Abdominal CT combined studies Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, National Quality Forum; “The evidence base indicates that a CT Thorax scan be performed either without or with contrast but not both.’ Simultaneous brain and sinus CT Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality, National Quality Measures Clearinghouse; “simultaneous CT sinus and brain imaging for headache without suspected complications is generally considered inappropriate” Thorax CT combined studies Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, National Quality Forum “The indiscriminate use of combined Thorax CT studies defined as those that are performed both without and with contrast agents for the evaluation of solid organs and body cavities represents a serious inefficiency of practice and a patient safety issue.” Open in a new tab Sources: U.S. Preventive Services Task Force ( uspreventiveservicestaskforce.org ); Choosing Wisely ( choosingwisely.org ); Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services ( cms.gov ); Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality ( ahrq.gov ); Charlesworth CJ, Meath TH, Schwartz AL, McConnell KJ. Comparison of low-value care in Medicaid vs commercially insured populations. JAMA internal medicine. 2016 Jul 1;176( 7 ):998–1004; Colla CH, Sequist TD, Rosenthal MB, Schpero WL, Gottlieb DJ, Morden NE. Use of non-indicated cardiac testing in lowrisk patients: Choosing Wisely. BMJ Qual Saf. 2014 Aug 5. Notes: † Guidelines updating PSA screening were initially released in Fall 2011, changing the grade of recommendation from I to D. It was updated, and the final recommendation changed from a D to C, in 2018 After our data were collected, federal guidelines for one low-value service, prostate-specific antigen (PSA) screening for prostate cancer, were updated from a grade D to a grade C, which removed them from low-value, instead reflecting that men should consult with their physicians. We leave it in the low-value category for this study, despite the recent update, as the recommendations against screening were in effect for nearly our entire study period. Additionally, we split low-value services into low-value screenings, done on asymptomatic individuals, and low-value tests for specific symptomatic concerns. We create this split for a couple of reasons. First, much of the existing evidence on low-value care in the commercial population has focused on low-value treatments for symptomatic concerns (e.g. imaging for uncomplicated back pain) and we wanted to generate estimates in this population that could be compared to others. Second, we are looking for changes in services concurrent with increases in high-value preventive screenings done on asymptomatic individuals. Thus, low-value screenings for asymptomatic individuals are more closely related, and perhaps may be differentially impacted, compared with those done for symptomatic concerns. We also measure preventive office visits, required once every two years for an average member of our population, and separate these visits from high-value preventive screening services. Preventive office visits are an important component of the the program intervention, both because they are a required service and because they are a referral point for other required and non-required services. Statistical Analyses We used a difference-in-differences research design to measure the change in likelihood of receiving a high- or low-value service after the implementation of the program relative to the likelihood of service use in a control group with stable benefit designs. For ease of interpretation, our primary specification uses linear probability models to estimate the likelihood of each outcome. ( 20 ) It is fully described in Appendix Exhibit 4 .( 17 ) We perform sensitivity analyses using the same dependent and explanatory variables using maximum likelihood estimation with a probit function. All models control for baseline differences between groups, year trends in utilization, age (in approximately 10-year categories), gender and dependent status. While the research design allows us to control for baseline differences in outcomes, it assumes that, absent treatment, these differences are constant over time. We test for these parallel trends both graphically and in formal regression analyses. Ideally, we would examine several years of pre-period data; we have only one year. We thus examine trends in high- and low-value service use in the months prior to the intervention. Appendix Exhibits 5 and 6 show the unadjusted percent of the program population and control sample that receive a high- or low-value service each month in the year prior to the intervention; the graphs show both population prevalence and the de-meaned prevalence (the overall mean for each group subtracted from the monthly prevalence).( 17 ) As shown in these graphs, the intervention population has consistently higher baseline utilization of both high- and low-value services than the control sample, with similar, slightly increasing trends. For high-value services, the de-meaned lines nearly overlap, suggesting very similar trends. For low-value services, the de-meaned trend line is slightly steeper for the control sample; if that trend continued through the post-period it would bias these estimates toward the null. Formal tests of the parallel trends assumption follow previous literature ( 21 )( 22 ) to assess differences in outcome trends in the pre-intervention period. In these tests, the magnitude of the interaction term between the time trend and the treatment group is less than 1 percentage point in all cases (right column, Appendix Exhibit 7 ), and most are not statistically significant.( 17 ) Finally, to explore a potential mechanism behind observed changes in service use, we decompose our main results into the portion associated with changes in receipt of preventive office visits and the portion associated with an increase in high- or low-value service use conditional on a preventive office visit. This decomposition is performed by computing counterfactual changes in high- and low-value service use that would have occurred in the post-intervention years if the percent of the population using a high- or low-value service conditional on a preventive visit had remained unchanged from baseline levels. We compare the counterfactual change to the actual change to compute the share attributable to changes in the prevalence of preventive visits. We offer further details of the approach in Appendix Exhibit 8 .( 17 ) Limitations This study has several limitations. First, while the voluntary nature of the program enrollment raises concerns of potential selection bias, the impact of this potential bias is mitigated as state employees who met inclusion criteria were included in the analysis regardless of the program participation. Second, our estimates relate to a single group of employees in one state. Thus, the estimates may have limited generalizability to other populations or other types of interventions. In extrapolating to other populations, we note that this population is similar to our control in baseline demographic characteristics. Extrapolating to other types of interventions may be more difficult. As preventive care was required to remain on the health plan with lower cost sharing, this intervention had a stronger nudge toward such care than interventions that rely solely on reduced cost sharing. However, the care delivery model is not different in the intervention state than in other states and we have no reason to think that other interventions that successfully induce demand for preventive care would not see similar effects, even if the magnitudes vary. Indeed, in previous studies, reductions in the cost of outpatient care were associated with increased use of inpatient services, implying economic complementarity between medical services is not unusual.( 23 )( 24 ) Third, our research design assumes no concurrent changes in the intervention state that may relate to our outcomes and bias results. We know of no other statewide interventions that occurred during the time period of our data collection, including in widespread changes to provider payment, that would have differentially affected the intervention state, though we are unable to formally test for this. The implementation of the Affordable Care Act, while not differentially impactful in this population may change interpretation of our results. The Act was signed into law in March 2010 just before our study period begins (July 2010). One provision of that law eliminated cost sharing for high-value preventive services for all new health plans, including those targeted by the the program intervention and potentially in our control group plans. Thus, a change in targeted high-value preventive services, and commensurate effects in low-value services, could reflect the role of participation requirements rather than of reductions in cost-sharing, and we cannot directly separate these effects. Last, the use of administrative medical claims to classify high- and low-value services is imprecise. While this method has advantages over other possible methods, notably the ability to easily look at a large population, claims may not capture nuances of medical interactions, and there may be variation in coding among providers. However, we do not think there are systematic differences that would bias estimates of changes in prevalence of high- and low-value services between our treatment and control samples over time. Results Baseline (Pre-the program) Characteristics The median age at baseline is about 42 for both the the program population and control group, and the control group has a slightly higher percentage of females ( Exhibit 2 ; also see Appendix Exhibit 9 )( 17 ). The the program population has a higher percentage of enrollees with a positive Charlson score, a measure of comorbidities and with a chronic condition. A high percent of both groups have a medical claim in the baseline year, and the the program population is more likely to have a claim. Exhibit 2: Descriptive Characteristics of the program Population and Control Group Sample Intervention Population Control Sample Female (%) 53.8 57.8 Age (Mean) 41.6 42.2 Dependent (%) 41.0 30.9 Charlson Score = 1 (%) 14.8 11.4 Charlson Score = 2 (%) 6.0 4.5 Chronic Condition (%) 42.3 39.6 Enrollees with any claim (%) 93.3 88.7 Number in each group 64,165 215,314 Open in a new tab Source: Authors’ analysis of baseline demographic and claims data from the intervention state and from the Truven MarketScan Commercial Claims and Encounters Database, July 2010–June 2011. Notes: Baseline is the plan year before the implementation of the Health Enhancement Program (the program). Chronic conditions measured include diabetes, heart disease, COPD, asthma, hypertension, and hyperlipidemia. All differences are significant at p <0.001. In the baseline year, about one-third of the the program population received at least one low-value service, compared with about one-fourth of those in the control group ( Exhibit 3 ). For both groups, the likelihood of receiving at least one high-value service was higher; about 60% in the the program population and 55% in the control group. Exhibit 3: Baseline Rates and Regression-Based Estimates of Changes in High- and Low-Value Services in Baseline and Post-Period Years Percent of Intervention Population and Control Sample Using Following Services Baseline Intervention Population Service Prevalence Baseline Control Sample Service Prevalence (%) Relative Percentage Point Change in Intervention Population: Year 1 Relative Percentage Point Change in Intervention Population: Year 2 Preventive Office Visits 53.0 36.9 13.5 ** 4.8 ** High-Value Service 59.8 55.0 11.0 ** 5.2 ** Low-Value Service 33.6 23.4 7.9 ** 2.7 ** Low-Value: Primary Preventive Services 28.8 18.5 8.8 ** 3.1 ** Low-Value: Referral for Symptomatic Concerns 7.8 6.8 -0.5 ** -0.5 ** Total Number (N) 64,165 215,314 Open in a new tab Source: Authors’ analysis of demographic and claims data from the intervention state and from the Truven MarketScan Commercial Claims and Encounters Database, July 2010–June 2013. Notes: Outcome measures: High-value services: colon cancer screening for individuals 50 and over, cervical cancer screening for women 21 and over, mammography for women beginning at age 35, lipid screening at age-specific intervals.Primary preventive low-value services: vitamin D screening, PSA screening, cervical cancer screening in women under 21, cardiac screening in asymptomatic individuals. Low-value referral for symptomatic concerns: imaging for uncomplicated headache, imaging for uncomplicated low back pain, electroencephalogram for headaches, CT scan for uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis, imaging for plantar fasciitis, head imaging for syncope, abdominal CT combined studies, simultaneous brain and sinus CT, thorax CT combined studies. Percentage point change are from a linear probability model with service use as the dependent variable and the interaction between post period and treatment group estimating the effect of interest. All models controlled for age (in approximately 10-year bands), gender, dependent status, Charlson comorbidity score, plan year and baseline differences between treatment and control group in outcome measures. Statistical significance refers to the p value at a level that allows us to reject the null hypothesis of no difference in the intervention population compared with the comparison group. ** p<=0.001 * p<=0.05 Change in High-Value and Low-Value Services After the the program intervention, the percent of the the program population who received at least one high- and low-value service increased ( Exhibit 4 ). These graphs show the percent of people who receive at least one high- or low-value service each quarter and annual averages across quarters. The increase in targeted high-value services was intended by the intervention. However, the percent of the population using low-value services shows similar increases, indicating spillover to those services. EXHIBIT 4. Open in a new tab Percent of the Intervention Population and Control sample receiving at least one high- or low-value service each calendar quarter prior to and after program implementation. SOURCE: Authors’ analysis of demographic and claims data from the intervention state and from the Truven MarketScan Commercial Claims and Encounters Database, July 2010–June 2013. NOTES: Each dot shows the percent of the the program population or control sample that received a high- or low-value service in each quarter. Lines show the mean percent of service use across quarters for each year: 1 year prior to the intervention and 2 years post-intervention. The vertical line shows the exact timing of the intervention, which occurred in July 2011, at the beginning of the 3rd calendar quarter in that year. The set of high- and low-value services used are noted in Exhibit 3 with more details in Appendix Exhibit 3 . The difference-in-difference estimates in Exhibit 3 confirm the significance of the changes apparent graphically. Relative to the control group, the percent of the program population receiving a high-value service in the first year of the program rose 11.0 percentage points (ppts), a relative 18% change, and increased 5.2 ppts in year 2, a relative 9% change ( Exhibit 3 ). Likewise, the percent of the program population who received a low-value service rose 7.9 percentage points in year 1, a 24% relative change, and 2.7 percentage points in year 2, an 8% relative change. Splitting low-value services into the types of low-value services used shows that the increase in low-value services is entirely driven by increases in the likelihood of a low-value primary preventive service. The percent of the population that receives a low-value primary preventive service increases by 8.8 percentage points, a relative 31%, in the first year and by 3.1 percentage points, relative 11%, in the second year ( Exhibit 3 ). By contrast, low value testing for symptomatic concerns actually declines slightly (0.5 percentage points) in each year. The magnitudes of these declines are much lower, as is the relative decline, about 6%. Full coefficients and statistics for each regression are in Appendix Exhibit 10 ( 17 ). The change in low-value service use varied by type of service, with PSA tests and cardiac imaging for low-risk asymptomatic individuals increasing most in year 1, and vitamin D tests and cardiac imaging increasing most in year 2 ( Appendix Exhibit 11 ).( 17 ) Results were similar in magnitude to our main OLS model using a probit function ( Appendix Exhibit 12 ).( 17 ) Association of High-Value and Low-Value Services with Preventive Care Visits In the decomposition of the change in service use attributable to the increase in preventive office visits, we find that more than half of the increase in high- and low-value service use is associated with an increase in preventive visits. Using counterfactual rates of the increase in high- and low-value service use that assume the likelihood of preventive visits increased and the rate of high- and low-value service use conditional on a preventive visit remains constant, we find an 8.1 ppt increase in likelihood of high-value service use in year 1 and a 2.9 ppt increase in year 2, representing 74% and 56% of the total increase respectively. ( Appendix Exhibit 8 )( 17 ). The rate of low-value service use increases 4.5 ppts in year 1 and 1.6 ppts in year 2, representing 57% and 56% of the total increase. Discussion This study is among the first to provide evidence that an enhancement of health insurance generosity specifically intended to improve uptake of high-value preventive services is associated with spillover effects on low-value services. Low-value service use increases were apparent for population-wide screenings often done in conjunction with primary care, such as screening for low vitamin D levels and abnormal PSA, and cardiac imaging in low-risk asymptomatic individuals. Relative increases in these low-value primary preventive services were similar to relative increases in high-value preventive services, suggesting that in this intervention, either providers or patients did not distinguish between high- and low-value preventive care. These findings are largely consistent with – but directionally opposite of– analogous research on high-deductible health plans showing that increases in consumer cost sharing reduce use of both high- and low-value services, and does not disproportionately affect low-value spending.( 25 )( 26 )( 27 ) The implication of these findings and our research is that neither benefit structure is targeted enough to ensure a more efficient mix of services. Our decomposition of results into high- and low-value services associated with a preventive visit suggests observed increases are often associated with an increase in preventive care visits. A key element of the intervention was changes in benefit design to induce demand for preventive office visits. In this case, it’s possible this element of the intervention did not translate into precisely targeted changes in utilization patterns. Further studies should examine the mechanisms underlying concurrent increases in high- and low-value care, and the role of preventive office visits or, potentially, other common service referral points. While our results showed an increase in low-value services in both program years, utilization increases were stronger in the first compared to the second year. Most of the measured high-value services, and preventive office visits, are not recommended every year (e.g. Pap tests, colonoscopies) so these results could reflect a natural wane as people completed their requirements and then were exempt in the second year. While this was one specific intervention, it has implications for efforts to increase demand for high-value services that may apply in many contexts. Many interventions promote preventive care and specifically preventive visits through cost-sharing structures, enhanced access to primary care, and alternative payment contracts tied to quality metrics. As innovations in benefit design promulgate and payments become more aligned with quality metrics, the likelihood is that initiatives aimed to encourage high-value care will become more commonplace and similar unwanted consequences may also result. Health system leaders and policymakers should consider designing programs that would reduce unwanted consequences, either through more precise targeting of specific high-value services or, preferably, by including deterrents to low-value care in benefit design and provider incentives. Additionally, assessment efforts should evaluate the impacts of consumer-focused interventions to change the service utilization on both high- and low-value services, regardless of how incentives are targeted. Our results should not be taken as a rebuke of aspirations to increase utilization of high-value medical care. As health care costs continue to rise, the need for a high-performing health system that encourages high-value care and discourages waste is greater than ever. Payment policy and benefit packages should be designed with both goals in mind, and assessed for their impact on overall efficiency of service use. Supplementary Material Supplementary Material NIHMS1693645-supplement-Supplementary_Material.pdf (296.3KB, pdf) Contributor Information Betsy Q. Cliff, Department of Health Management and Policy, University of Michigan School of Public Health, Ann Arbor MI Richard A. Hirth, Department of Health Management and Policy, University of Michigan School of Public Health, Ann Arbor MI A.Mark Fendrick, Department of Internal Medicine, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor MI. End Notes 1. Chernew M, Schwartz JS, Fendrick AM. Reconciling Prevention and Value in the Health Care System. Health Affairs Blog: Health Policy Lab. http://healthaffairs.org/blog/2015/03/11/reconciling-prevention-and-value-in-the-health-care-system.Published 2015. March 11. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Fedewa SA, Goodman M, Flanders WD, Han X, Smith RAM, Ward E, Doubeni CA, Sauer AG, Jemal A. Elimination of cost-sharing and receipt of screening for colorectal and breast cancer. Cancer. 2015. September 15;121(18):3272–80. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Eskew PM, Klink K. Dir